WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:11.900
Dark patterns are features of interface design

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:16.300
crafted to trick users into doing things that they might not want to do,

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:19.100
but which benefit the business in question.

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:23.500
Here's an example: have you ever tried to delete your Amazon account?

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:25.100
Here's the Amazon homepage.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:27.100
What's the first thing you might think to do?

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:30.300
The obvious place to look is the account drop-down, here.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.900
Once you're in here, you look around -- it's a lot of information.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:38.500
But if I'm interested in deleting my account, I'd think that 'Your Account' is probably a good place to go.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:40.820
Once I'm on this page, there's a lot more information:

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:44.800
'Payment options', 'Login &amp; security' and a bunch of stuff down here.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:48.000
Unfortunately, you could click every link on your account page

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.400
but none would deliver you to a place where you could actually delete it, because it's not here.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:56.800
In order to actually delete your Amazon account, you have to go all the way down to the bottom of the page,

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:59.700
and under 'Let Us Help You' click 'Help'.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.420
Once you're in here, you have to navigate to 'Need More Help?'

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:06.100
because, y'know, putting it on this page would just be too easy.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:09.600
Then click 'Contact Us'.
(This is where it starts to get ridiculous.)

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.520
It's still nowhere to be seen, but of the four options on the top that you want help with,

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:17.100
click 'Prime or Something else'.
(You want the 'Something else'.)

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:20.000
In this 'Tell us more about your issue' drop-down,

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.100
there's still nothing that suggests account deletion;

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:25.700
you just have to know to click 'Login and security',

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:27.900
and then in a second drop-down,

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:31.200
there it is, the magic button -- 'Close my account'.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:35.200
Except in order to actually do that, you now have to have a chat conversation with an Amazon associate

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.900
who's going to tell you all the reasons account deletion is a bad idea.

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:43.000
See, you can't delete the account yourself; 
they have to do it for you.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:47.800
This is a dark pattern: a crappy user experience that intentionally makes it difficult,

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:49.800
almost impossible without help,

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.100
to do something that hurts Amazon.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:57.600
UX specialist Harry Brignull categorizes the specific kind of dark pattern as a 'roach motel':

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:03.600
a design that makes it very easy for you to get into a situation, but very hard to get out.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:07.600
Brignull's actually the one who coined the term 'dark pattern' in 2010

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:10.900
and he's been cataloguing and lecturing about the issue ever since.

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:13.400
Many of these dark patterns we're all familiar with;

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:16.400
I only have to search my email for a few seconds to find one.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:20.400
For example, here: I'm getting spam emails from Architectural Digest.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.600
I scroll down, and...

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:26.700
Look at that. This is a mess, but it's a mess on purpose.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:30.480
The unsubscribe link is here but it's devilishly hard to see.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:35.000
That's because it's the same font and virtually the same color as the rest of the fine print.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.600
Here's another dark pattern that uses color to misdirect:

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:42.100
over at the UserTestingBlog, Jennifer Derome points out that the mobile game Two Dots

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:45.600
carries you through the experience by offering green buttons.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:50.000
A green button to start the game, a green button to pick a level, a green button to start the level

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.700
and three green buttons to continue to the next level, and so on.

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:57.040
But once you lose a level, the color scheme changes.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:00.500
The first screen button you see leads you right to an in-app purchase,

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:04.800
while the continue button is just a little X that blends into the larger element.

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:09.900
(I wonder how many people clicked 'Buy Moves' reflexively as a Pavlovian response.)

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:12.500
Now to be fair, this is a pretty benign dark pattern,

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:17.600
but it shows how companies can use something as simple as color to trick you into doing what they want.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:20.100
On the more egregious end of the spectrum, you have stuff like

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:24.600
this banner ad for Chatmost, which is made to look like it has a speck of dust on it,

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:28.700
causing people to brush it away and accidentally click the link.

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:32.800
Or you have sites like Booking.com where they do everything in their power

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:35.900
to increase the urgency of a purchase,

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:42.500
going so far as to alert you in big red notifications of the hotel rooms that you *just* missed.

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:46.300
(I mean, you better book now. You don't want to be left behind again, do you?)

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:50.300
Every once in a while, a company goes a little too far and actually breaks the law.

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:54.100
Remember when you used to get spam with all those LinkedIn invites from friends?

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:57.700
Well, that was because of a confusing dark pattern on their 'Add Contacts' page

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:01.300
which allowed LinkedIn to scoop up people's email contacts

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:04.600
and send them messages repeatedly without their consent.

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:10.100
In a comprehensive blog post, Dan Schlosser showed how LinkedIn tried to trick users eight times

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:14.100
in their sign up and onboarding procedures into surrendering their email contacts.

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:16.900
Unfortunately for LinkedIn, this proved to be a step too far;

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:19.000
users filed a legal challenge claiming that

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:23.100
sending multiple unwarranted emails hurt their professional reputation.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:29.500
LinkedIn settled the dispute for $13 million, which came out in the end to about $10 per user.

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:32.600
It's rare for dark patterns face consequences like these.

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:35.700
Mostly, they stay *just* on the right side of the law,

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:40.900
understanding that it's hard to legislate around the psychological tricks of UX design.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:43.600
Everything on the internet is fighting for your attention,

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:48.800
but there's a difference between those who are taking the time to build trust and loyalty

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:52.900
and the special offer you clicked which actually enrolled you in a monthly subscription,

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:58.800
or the social network that dark-patterned you into letting it sell data that you didn't even know it had.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:03.200
Some of the responsibility is on us but some is on design, too.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:06.600
And it's not the fault of the designers -- they're just doing what they're tasked to do,

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:09.600
knowing full well that if they don't, others will.

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:14.800
As Brignull says, "Our best defense against the dark patterns is to be aware of them,

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:17.200
and shame the companies who utilize them."

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:22.900
Design is what mediates our interaction with the Internet: it's the language we read it in.

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:27.600
It's not too much to ask that that language be comprehensible and honest.

